# Introduction # {#intro}

Every application scenario may require different sets of properties to properly describe a given <a href="../#terminology">digital object</a>; the FDP therefore supports deployment-defined <a href="../#terminology">metadata schema</a>s.
<a href="../core/">FDP Core</a> requires schemas to be expressed in SHACL and to be retrievable; this module specifies how schemas are managed: added, edited, deleted, and extended.

Terminology is defined in the <a href="../#terminology">umbrella document</a>.
The key words MUST, MUST NOT, SHOULD, SHOULD NOT, and MAY are to be interpreted as described in [[!RFC2119]].

# Conformance # {#conformance-class}

This document defines the conformance class identified by:

<pre>
https://specs.fairdatapoint.org/v2.0/conformance/schemas
</pre>

A <a href="../#terminology">deployment</a> conforms to FDP Schema Management if and only if it conforms to <a href="../core/">FDP Core</a> and satisfies all MUST-level requirements in this document.

: <dfn>SCH-01</dfn>
:: A deployment claiming this conformance class MUST also conform to the FDP Core conformance class of the same suite version.

# Schemas as resources # {#schema-resources}

: <dfn>SCH-02</dfn>
:: Every metadata schema in use by the deployment MUST be exposed as an addressable resource, retrievable per Core's retrieval and content-negotiation requirements, with the SHACL shapes graph as its representation.

: <dfn>SCH-03</dfn>
:: The deployment MUST expose a schema <a href="../#terminology">container</a> listing all its schemas via <code>ldp:contains</code>, and that container MUST be discoverable from the FDP's own metadata record.

Note: Exposing the schema catalogue through the same LDP containment mechanism used for metadata records keeps the client model uniform: one navigation mechanism for everything the FDP serves.

# Creating and modifying schemas # {#create-modify}

: <dfn>SCH-04</dfn>
:: Creation of a schema MUST be supported via an HTTP POST on the schema container, with the SHACL shapes graph as the request body, accepting at least Turtle and JSON-LD; the deployment MUST verify that the submitted graph is syntactically valid SHACL and that it satisfies Core requirement CORE-15 (target class is <code>dcat:Resource</code> or a subclass); a violation MUST be answered with status code <code>422</code> and a report of the violation.

: <dfn>SCH-05</dfn>
:: On successful creation, the deployment MUST return <code>201</code> with a <code>Location</code> header carrying the schema's IRI and MUST add the schema to the schema container.

: <dfn>SCH-06</dfn>
:: Modification of a schema MUST be supported via an HTTP PUT on the schema's IRI, as a full replacement, subject to the same verification as [=SCH-04=].

: <dfn>SCH-07</dfn>
:: Schemas SHOULD be versioned: when a schema is modified, the deployment SHOULD keep the previous version retrievable at a version-specific IRI, so that records whose <a href="../#terminology">profile</a> reference points to the earlier version continue to resolve to the schema they were validated against.

# Deleting schemas # {#delete}

: <dfn>SCH-08</dfn>
:: Deletion of a schema MUST be supported via an HTTP DELETE on the schema's IRI; the deployment MUST refuse, with status code <code>409</code>, the deletion of a schema that is referenced as the profile of any existing metadata record.

: <dfn>SCH-09</dfn>
:: The deployment MUST refuse, with status code <code>409</code>, the deletion or the weakening of the two mandatory base schemas — the FAIR Data Point schema and the Catalog schema — where weakening means any modification under which metadata content satisfying the modified schema would violate a Core requirement.

# Extending the base schemas # {#extension}

Deployments commonly need richer descriptions than the base schemas require — additional properties, stricter cardinalities, controlled vocabularies for specific fields.

: <dfn>SCH-10</dfn>
:: A deployment MAY extend the base schemas and MAY define schemas for additional record types; an extension of a base schema MUST preserve every constraint of the base schema (extensions may add constraints, never remove or relax them).

: <dfn>SCH-11</dfn>
:: Access control for the operations in this module MUST follow the same interface behaviour as Records Management (requirements REC-14 and REC-15 in <a href="../records/">FDP Records Management</a>): protection possible, <code>401</code>/<code>403</code> semantics.

<div class=issue>
**Open question — revalidation semantics when a schema changes.**
When a schema is modified ([=SCH-06=]), existing records that were valid against the previous version may be invalid against the new one.
This draft deliberately does not require automatic revalidation, because the cost on large deployments can be substantial and rejection of previously accepted records would be hostile to data holders.
Options for the community group:
(a) no obligation — records keep their validity status until next modified (the current draft's implicit position);
(b) lazy revalidation — a record is revalidated against the current schema whenever it is next written;
(c) eager revalidation with flagging — the deployment revalidates affected records and exposes a per-record conformance status, following the accept-and-flag posture that FAIR Discovery applies on the harvesting side;
(d) mandatory schema versioning — turn [=SCH-07=] into a MUST, so that a record's profile reference always pins the exact schema version it conforms to and revalidation is never implicit.
Options (c) and (d) combine well and would make FDP-side behaviour symmetrical with the conformance reporting harvesters produce.
</div>

# Requirements summary # {#requirements-summary}

<table class="def">
    <caption>FDP Schema Management requirements</caption>
    <thead>
        <tr><th>ID</th><th>Level</th><th>Summary</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>[=SCH-01=]</td><td>MUST</td><td>Core conformance is a prerequisite</td></tr>
        <tr><td>[=SCH-02=]</td><td>MUST</td><td>Every schema is an addressable, negotiable resource</td></tr>
        <tr><td>[=SCH-03=]</td><td>MUST</td><td>Schema container discoverable from the FDP record</td></tr>
        <tr><td>[=SCH-04=]</td><td>MUST</td><td>Create via POST; SHACL and target-class verification; 422 on violation</td></tr>
        <tr><td>[=SCH-05=]</td><td>MUST</td><td>201 + Location; schema added to the container</td></tr>
        <tr><td>[=SCH-06=]</td><td>MUST</td><td>Modify via PUT, verified as on creation</td></tr>
        <tr><td>[=SCH-07=]</td><td>SHOULD</td><td>Version schemas; previous versions stay resolvable</td></tr>
        <tr><td>[=SCH-08=]</td><td>MUST</td><td>Refuse (409) deleting a schema in use</td></tr>
        <tr><td>[=SCH-09=]</td><td>MUST</td><td>Refuse (409) deleting/weakening the mandatory base schemas</td></tr>
        <tr><td>[=SCH-10=]</td><td>MUST</td><td>Extensions preserve base-schema constraints</td></tr>
        <tr><td>[=SCH-11=]</td><td>MUST</td><td>Access control per Records Management interface behaviour</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
